{
  "label": "convex",
  "explanation": "Let f(x)=1/((7/4)+e^x) with x∈[1,2]. Set g(x)=(7/4)+e^x>0. Then f'(x)=-(e^x)/g(x)^2 and f''(x)=e^x(e^x-7/4)/g(x)^3. For x∈[1,2], e^x≥e>7/4, so e^x(e^x-7/4)>0 while g(x)^3>0, hence f''(x)>0 on the whole interval. Therefore f is convex on [1,2] (and not concave)."
}

{
  "label": "concave",
  "explanation": "For f(x)=1/((7/4)+e^x), let g(x)=(7/4)+e^x>0. Then f'(x)=-e^x/g(x)^2 and f''(x)=e^x(e^x-7/4)/g(x)^3. On x∈[-2,-1], e^x∈[e^{-2},e^{-1}]≈[0.135,0.368]<7/4, so e^x-7/4<0 and hence f''(x)<0 throughout. Therefore f is concave on the given interval."
}

{
  "label": "convex",
  "explanation": "Let a=11/8. The function is f(x)=log(1+exp(ax))-(121/1024)x^2 on x∈[-1,1]. For g(x)=log(1+exp(ax)), g''(x)=a^2·exp(ax)/(1+exp(ax))^2. The quadratic term has second derivative d^2/dx^2 (-(121/1024)x^2)=-(121/512). Hence\nf''(x)=a^2·exp(ax)/(1+exp(ax))^2-121/512 = (121/512)·(8·exp(ax)/(1+exp(ax))^2-1).\nOn x≥0, y=exp(ax) increases with x and h(y)=8y/(1+y)^2 has derivative h'(y)=8(1-y)/(1+y)^3<0 for y>1, so f''(x) is minimized on [0,1] at x=1; by symmetry f'' is minimized on [-1,1] at x=±1. At x=1, with t=exp(11/8)≈3.95, we get 8t/(1+t)^2≈8·3.95/4.95^2≈1.29>1, so f''(±1)>0. Therefore f''(x)>0 for all x∈[-1,1], so f is convex on the interval (and not concave)."
}

{
  "label": "neither",
  "explanation": "On [-1,1] the function is the even polynomial f(x)=(1/90)x^10-(13/700)x^8+(241/18750)x^6-(78/15625)x^4+(4917791/3200000000)x^2, so f is twice differentiable. Its second derivative is f''(x)=x^8-(26/25)x^6+(241/625)x^4-(936/15625)x^2+4917791/1600000000. Then f''(0)=4917791/1600000000>0, but for x^2=2/5 (i.e., x=±sqrt(2/5) in [-1,1]) we get f''(±sqrt(2/5))= -243169/1600000000<0. Since f'' changes sign on the interval, f is neither convex nor concave on [-1,1]."
}

